Category-Theoretic Discovery of Quantum Error-Correcting Codes

A 2-Categorical Framework for Compositional Fault-Tolerant Architectures

Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, kimi 

Abstract

Quantum error correction is the central bottleneck preventing large-scale fault-tolerant computation. Recent breakthroughs in quantum low-density parity-check (qLDPC) codes have overturned long‑standing assumptions: asymptotically good codes now exist, and transversal non‑Clifford gates have been demonstrated on codes with polynomial distance. Despite these advances, the discovery of new codes remains an artisanal process, driven by isolated homological or algebraic insights with no systematic compositional framework.

This dissertation introduces the first higher‑categorical theory of quantum code discovery. We define a strict 2‑category, 2QEC, whose objects are CSS stabilizer codes represented as chain complexes over \mathbb{F}_2, whose 1‑morphisms are fault‑tolerant code transformations given by compatible chain maps, and whose 2‑morphisms are equivalences between transformation pathways encoded as symplectic chain homotopies. We prove that the construction is a strict 2‑category and define a faithful 2‑functor from the 2‑category of length‑2 chain complexes into 2QEC, converting homological operations into composable code morphisms.

This categorical foundation reduces the code discovery problem from an exponential search over the space of all stabilizer codes to a structured traversal of hom‑categories. We formulate a sound dependent type system in Lean 4 that statically guarantees the preservation of logical qubit count and code distance, extending the recently developed Lean‑QEC distance‑certification pipeline to categorical constructions. On the theoretical side, we prove that the balanced product functor preserves the property of admitting transversal non‑Clifford gates when the input complexes carry compatible cup‑product structures, thereby giving a unified categorical explanation of recent breakthroughs by Zhu (2026) and Lin (2024). We also put forward a concrete, falsifiable conjecture—Functorial Distance Amplification—that the balanced product of codes with linear distance yields distance that is superlinear in the block length.

The mathematical core of the dissertation is self‑contained: all definitions, theorems, and proofs are presented rigorously, and no part of the argument depends on machine‑learning components. The result is a formal framework that re‑expresses the construction of fault‑tolerant quantum codes as navigation through a higher category, opening the door to automated, formally verified discovery of qLDPC architectures.

