Chapter 5

A Dependent Type System for Code Morphisms

We present a dependent type theory in the style of Lean 4 that statically guarantees the preservation of logical qubit count and code distance for every well‑typed composition of morphisms. The type system acts as a static filter: only those chain maps that are accompanied by formal proofs of parameter preservation can be used in the compositional construction.

5.1 Refinement Types for Quantum Codes

Definition 5.1 (Code type).
For natural numbers n, k, d \in \mathbb{N}, we define the dependent type

\operatorname{\texttt{Code}}(n, k, d) \;\;:=\;\;
\Bigl\{ (C_\bullet, \pi) \;\Bigm|\;
C_\bullet \in \mathbf{2QEC},\;
\pi : (\dim (C_\bullet)_0 = n) \times (\dim H_1(C_\bullet) = k) \times (\operatorname{dist}(C_\bullet) \ge d) \Bigr\}.

Here \operatorname{dist}(C_\bullet) denotes the code distance as defined homologically in Section 3.2. The triple \pi is a term of the product proposition type, i.e., a formal proof of the three parameter conditions.

Definition 5.2 (Morphism type).
Let Q = (C_Q, \pi_Q) : \operatorname{\texttt{Code}}(n_Q, k_Q, d_Q) and R = (C_R, \pi_R) : \operatorname{\texttt{Code}}(n_R, k_R, d_R). The type of performance‑preserving morphisms from Q to R is

\operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q, R) \;\;:=\;\;
\Bigl\{ f \in \operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbf{2QEC}}(C_Q, C_R) \;\Bigm|\;
(k_Q \le k_R) \land (d_Q \le d_R) \Bigr\}.

Note that the condition is a proposition: it does not require the morphism to explain why the distance is preserved, only that the target distance is at least the source distance.

5.2 Typing Rules

We give the rules of the type system in natural deduction style. The judgement

\Gamma \vdash f : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q, R)

asserts that f is a well‑typed morphism under context \Gamma.

Rule (Id).

\frac{}{\Gamma \vdash \mathrm{id}_Q : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q, Q)}

Justification: k_Q \le k_Q and d_Q \le d_Q are reflexivity.

Rule (Comp).

\frac{\Gamma \vdash f : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q, R) \qquad
\Gamma \vdash g : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(R, S)}
{\Gamma \vdash g \circ f : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q, S)}

Justification: From the premises we have k_Q \le k_R, k_R \le k_S and d_Q \le d_R, d_R \le d_S. By transitivity of the natural order, k_Q \le k_S and d_Q \le d_S. Moreover, the composition of chain maps is a chain map.

Rule (Conv).

\frac{\Gamma \vdash f : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q, R) \qquad
\Gamma \vdash \eta : f \Rightarrow g \;\; (\text{2‑cell in } \mathbf{2QEC})}
{\Gamma \vdash g : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q, R)}

Justification: The existence of a chain homotopy \eta between f and g implies that f and g induce the same maps on homology H_1 and on cohomology H^1. Hence the logical qubit count and the logical operator structure are invariant under f \leadsto g. The distance inequality d_Q \le d_R is a property of the codes Q and R, not of the particular map between them; thus it remains valid for g. Consequently, g is also performance‑preserving.

5.3 Soundness

Theorem 5.3 (Soundness).
If \Gamma \vdash f : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q, R) is derivable in the above system, then the underlying chain map f : C_Q \to C_R satisfies the following physical interpretation:

1. (Logical embedding) The induced map f_* : H_1(C_Q) \to H_1(C_R) is injective. In particular, the number of logical qubits is non‑decreasing: k_Q \le k_R.
2. (Distance preservation) The code distance of R is at least that of Q: \operatorname{dist}(C_R) \ge \operatorname{dist}(C_Q).

Proof.  By induction on the derivation.

· Base case (Id). The identity map induces the identity on homology, which is injective, and \operatorname{dist}(C_Q) \ge \operatorname{dist}(C_Q) holds trivially.
· Induction step (Comp). Suppose f : Q \to R and g : R \to S satisfy the properties. The composition g_* \circ f_* : H_1(C_Q) \to H_1(C_S) is injective as the composition of two injective maps. The distance bound follows by transitivity: \operatorname{dist}(C_S) \ge \operatorname{dist}(C_R) \ge \operatorname{dist}(C_Q).
· Induction step (Conv). Suppose f \in \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q, R) and \eta : f \Rightarrow g. Chain homotopic maps induce equal maps on homology: f_* = g_* : H_1(C_Q) \to H_1(C_R). Hence injectivity of f_* implies injectivity of g_*. The distance bound \operatorname{dist}(C_R) \ge \operatorname{dist}(C_Q) is a proposition that does not depend on which representative of the homotopy class is chosen; it holds for g because it holds for R as a code. ∎

Remark 5.4.  The injectivity condition in Theorem 5.3(1) is not automatically enforced by the type \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q, R) as defined; the type only guarantees the inequality k_Q \le k_R. For the soundness proof to claim injectivity, one must either strengthen the definition of \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}} to include a proof of injectivity on homology, or note that many natural code constructions (hypergraph product, balanced product) yield chain maps that are injective on homology by construction. In a fully formalised Lean 4 implementation, the type \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q, R) would include an explicit proof term for the homology injection. The present formulation keeps the type system lightweight, delegating the injectivity proof to the specific functor libraries.

Corollary 5.5 (Type safety for code discovery).  Any sequence of morphisms composed according to the typing rules produces a code whose parameters are guaranteed to be no worse than those of the initial seed code. Thus the type system provides static correctness guarantees for the categorical discovery pipeline.

5.4 Integration with Formal Verification

The type \operatorname{\texttt{Code}}(n,k,d) is a refinement type: a code is a chain complex together with a machine‑checkable proof of its parameters. In the Lean 4 implementation, the distance condition \operatorname{dist}(C_\bullet) \ge d is certified using the SAT‑based distance lower bound engine of Lean‑QEC (Ehatamm et al., 2026). The typing rules ensure that these certifications compose.

Lemma 5.6 (Composability of distance certificates).
Let f : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q, R) and g : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(R, S) be well‑typed morphisms whose underlying chain maps are synthetically constructed by the functors of Chapter 3. Then the distance certificate of Q and the typing derivations together yield a formal proof of \operatorname{dist}(C_S) \ge \operatorname{dist}(C_Q) without re‑certifying C_S from scratch.

Proof sketch.  The typing derivation provides the chain of inequalities d_S \ge d_R \ge d_Q. Each inequality is either an axiom from the functor library (e.g. “the hypergraph product preserves distance”) or a transitivity step. The distance axioms for the functors are proved separately in Lean 4 (Chapter 6). The typing rule (Comp) then combines these into a formal proof term. ∎

