Chapter 7

Transversal Non‑Clifford Gates via Functorial Products

We demonstrate that the balanced product functor preserves the property of admitting a transversal CCZ gate, provided the input complexes are equipped with compatible cup products. This gives a unified categorical explanation of the recent breakthroughs by Zhu (2026) and Lin (2024).

7.1 Cup Products on Chain Complexes

Definition 7.1 (Cup product).
Let C_\bullet \in \mathbf{2QEC}. A cup product on C_\bullet is a bilinear map

\smile : C_0 \times C_0 \longrightarrow C_0

satisfying the Leibniz rule

\partial^T (x \smile y) = \partial^T x \smile y + x \smile \partial^T y \qquad (\forall x,y \in C_0), \tag{7.1}

and supercommutativity x \smile y = y \smile x.
The map \smile induces a graded commutative algebra structure on the cohomology H^1(C) = \ker \partial^T / \operatorname{im} \partial, because (7.1) guarantees that \smile descends to cohomology.

Definition 7.2 (Triple cup product).
The triple cup product is the map \smile_3 : C_0 \times C_0 \times C_0 \to C_0 defined by (x,y,z) \mapsto (x \smile y) \smile z.

Definition 7.3 (Transversal CCZ gate).
A CSS code C admits a transversal CCZ gate if there exist three cohomology classes [\alpha],[\beta],[\gamma] \in H^1(C) such that on the physical qubits (indexed by a basis of C_0) the triple cup product evaluates to a non‑trivial logical operator:

\sum_{i=1}^{n} (\alpha_i \beta_i \gamma_i) \equiv 1 \pmod 2,

where \alpha_i,\beta_i,\gamma_i are the coefficients of the representative vectors. The physical CCZ gate is then applied on triples of qubits for which the tensor product of these vectors has support; transversality follows because the gate acts qubit‑wise.

7.2 Balanced Product of Complexes with Cup Products

Theorem 7.4 (Inheritance of the cup product).
Let G be a finite group and let A, B be G‑chain complexes equipped with G‑invariant cup products \smile_A, \smile_B:

(g\!\cdot\!x) \smile_A (g\!\cdot\!y) = x \smile_A y \qquad (\forall g\in G,\; x,y\in A_0),

and similarly for B. Then the balanced product complex C = A \boxtimes_G B inherits a well‑defined cup product

\smile_C : C_0 \times C_0 \longrightarrow C_0, \qquad
(x \otimes y) \smile_C (x' \otimes y') = (x \smile_A x') \otimes (y \smile_B y'),

which satisfies the Leibniz rule (7.1) and is supercommutative.

Proof.  Bilinearity and supercommutativity are immediate. To check that the map descends to the quotient, note that if x\otimes y \sim x\!\cdot\!g \otimes g^{-1}\!\cdot\!y, then

(x\!\cdot\!g \smile_A x') \otimes (g^{-1}\!\cdot\!y \smile_B y')
= (x \smile_A x') \otimes (y \smile_B y')

by G‑invariance of the cup products. Hence \smile_C is well defined.

We verify the Leibniz rule. Write u = x\otimes y, v = x'\otimes y'. Using the block form \partial_C^T = \begin{pmatrix} \partial_A^T \otimes I_B \\ I_A \otimes \partial_B^T \end{pmatrix}, compute

\partial_C^T(u \smile_C v) =
\begin{pmatrix}
\partial_A^T(x \smile_A x') \otimes (y \smile_B y') \\
(x \smile_A x') \otimes \partial_B^T(y \smile_B y')
\end{pmatrix}.

Apply the Leibniz rules on A and B:

\partial_A^T(x \smile_A x') = \partial_A^T x \smile_A x' + x \smile_A \partial_A^T x',\\
\partial_B^T(y \smile_B y') = \partial_B^T y \smile_B y' + y \smile_B \partial_B^T y'.

Thus

\partial_C^T(u \smile_C v) =
\begin{pmatrix}
(\partial_A^T x \smile_A x') \otimes (y \smile_B y') + (x \smile_A \partial_A^T x') \otimes (y \smile_B y') \\
(x \smile_A x') \otimes (\partial_B^T y \smile_B y') + (x \smile_A x') \otimes (y \smile_B \partial_B^T y')
\end{pmatrix}.

On the other hand,

\partial_C^T u \smile_C v =
\begin{pmatrix}
(\partial_A^T x) \otimes y \\
x \otimes (\partial_B^T y)
\end{pmatrix}
\smile_C (x' \otimes y')
=
\begin{pmatrix}
(\partial_A^T x \smile_A x') \otimes (y \smile_B y') \\
(x \smile_A x') \otimes (\partial_B^T y \smile_B y')
\end{pmatrix},

u \smile_C \partial_C^T v =
(x \otimes y) \smile_C
\begin{pmatrix}
(\partial_A^T x') \otimes y' \\
x' \otimes (\partial_B^T y')
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
(x \smile_A \partial_A^T x') \otimes (y \smile_B y') \\
(x \smile_A x') \otimes (y \smile_B \partial_B^T y')
\end{pmatrix}.

Adding these two expressions yields exactly \partial_C^T(u \smile_C v). Hence (7.1) holds. ∎

7.3 Transversal CCZ via the Balanced Product

Theorem 7.5 (Preservation of transversal CCZ).
Suppose A and B admit transversal CCZ gates realised by triple cup products of cohomology classes [\alpha],[\beta],[\gamma] \in H^1(A) and [\alpha'],[\beta'],[\gamma'] \in H^1(B), and that the cup products are G‑invariant. Then the balanced product code C = A \boxtimes_G B admits a transversal CCZ gate given by the class

[\alpha \otimes \alpha'] \smile_C [\beta \otimes \beta'] \smile_C [\gamma \otimes \gamma'] \in H^1(C).

Proof.  By the Künneth formula for cohomology with group actions, the classes [\alpha \otimes \alpha'] are well defined in H^1(C) because the group acts freely. The physical transversal gate is applied on qubits indexed by the basis of C_0 = (A_0 \otimes B_0)/{\sim} with coefficients given by the tensor products of the representatives. The triple cup product evaluates to

\sum_{(i,j)} (\alpha_i \beta_i \gamma_i) \otimes (\alpha'_j \beta'_j \gamma'_j)
= \left(\sum_i \alpha_i \beta_i \gamma_i\right) \otimes \left(\sum_j \alpha'_j \beta'_j \gamma'_j\right)
= 1 \otimes 1 = 1 \pmod 2,

because each factor separately sums to 1 by hypothesis. Hence the operator is a logical CCZ. Transversality follows from the fact that the gate acts on each tensor‑product pair of physical qubits independently. ∎

Corollary 7.6.  The balanced product of two codes that each support a transversal CCZ gate yields a larger code that also supports a transversal CCZ gate. This functorial mechanism lifts fault‑tolerant non‑Clifford operations to composed architectures, providing a unified categorical explanation of the results of Zhu (2026) and Lin (2024).

