Chapter 8

The Central Conjecture: Functorial Distance Amplification

We formulate a precise, falsifiable conjecture on the distance behaviour of the balanced product functor. As evidence, we fully analyse the balanced product of two repetition codes, showing a polynomial amplification from constant to linear distance.

8.1 Statement of the Conjecture

Conjecture 8.1 (Functorial Distance Amplification).
There exist universal constants c > 0 and \alpha \ge 0 such that the following holds.
Let A, B be G‑chain complexes in \mathbf{2QEC} whose associated CSS codes have linear distance, i.e.

d(A) \ge \delta \dim A_0, \qquad d(B) \ge \delta \dim B_0

for some \delta > 0. Assume that the finite group G acts freely on A and B, and that the complexes satisfy the following cohomology expansion property:

\operatorname{rank} \bigl( H^1(A) \otimes_{\mathbb{F}_2[G]} H^1(B) \to H^1(A \boxtimes_G B) \bigr) \ge \eta \dim H^1(A) \dim H^1(B)

for some \eta > 0. Then the balanced product code C = A \boxtimes_G B satisfies

d(C) \;\ge\; c \cdot \min\bigl(d(A),\, d(B)\bigr) \cdot (\dim C_0)^{\alpha}.

Remark 8.2.  The expansion condition is known to hold for the balanced product of two expander codes (Panteleev–Kalachev 2022, Dinur et al. 2022), which yield linear distance (\alpha = 0 in that case). The conjecture posits that by choosing the factors to be more structured (e.g. with cup products), one can achieve \alpha > 0, i.e. the distance grows superlinearly in the product length.

8.2 The Repetition Code Example

We provide an explicit computation that supports the conjecture. Let G = \mathbb{Z}/\ell\mathbb{Z} act by cyclic shifts.

Definition 8.3 (Repetition code complex).
For an even integer \ell = 2m, the repetition code complex A is defined by

A_1 = \mathbb{F}_2^\ell,\qquad A_0 = \mathbb{F}_2^\ell,

with boundary \partial : A_1 \to A_0 given in the standard basis by the matrix

\partial_{ij} = \begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } j = i \text{ or } j = i+1 \pmod \ell,\\
0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Equivalently, \partial(e_i) = e_i + e_{i+1} for i \in \mathbb{Z}/\ell (with e_{\ell+1}=e_1). One checks that \partial\partial^T = (I+S)(I+S^{-1}) = 2I + S + S^{-1} = 0 over \mathbb{F}_2 because 2=0 and S+S^{-1}=0 for even \ell. The associated CSS code has X‑stabilisers given by the rows of \partial and Z‑stabilisers by the rows of \partial^T. Its parameters are well known.

Lemma 8.4 (Distance of the repetition code).  For even \ell, d(A) = 2.

Proof.  The X‑distance is the minimum weight of a vector in \ker \partial \setminus \operatorname{im} \partial^T. The kernel of \partial consists of even‑weight vectors with all components equal; the all‑ones vector \mathbf{1} has weight \ell and is in \ker \partial. Vectors of weight 2 with two adjacent ones are in \ker \partial but not in the image of \partial^T. The Z‑distance is the minimum weight of a vector in \ker \partial^T \setminus \operatorname{im} \partial. The all‑ones vector is also in \ker \partial^T and has weight \ell; vectors of weight 1 are not in \ker \partial^T. The smallest weight of a non‑trivial logical operator is thus 2, achieved by a weight‑2 X‑logical operator. ∎

Theorem 8.5 (Balanced product of two repetition codes).
Let G = \mathbb{Z}/\ell\mathbb{Z} act freely by cyclic shifts on the repetition code complex A of even length \ell. Then the balanced product C = A \boxtimes_G A has parameters

n_C = \ell,\qquad k_C = 2,\qquad d_C = \ell.

Consequently,

d(C) = \frac{1}{2} \cdot d(A) \cdot \dim C_0 = \ell.

Proof.  The balanced product complex is computed explicitly. Write the basis of A_0 as \{e_i\} with i \in \mathbb{Z}/\ell. The quotient A_0 \otimes_{\mathbb{F}_2 G} A_0 identifies e_i \otimes e_j \sim e_{i+k} \otimes e_{j+k} for all k. Hence a basis for C_0 is given by the set \{e_i \otimes e_0 : i \in \mathbb{Z}/\ell\}; thus \dim C_0 = \ell. The boundary operator \partial_C acts on a basis vector e_i \otimes e_0 by

\partial_C(e_i \otimes e_0) = (e_i+e_{i+1})\otimes e_0 + e_i \otimes (e_0+e_1).

Under the quotient, the second term becomes e_i \otimes e_0 + e_i \otimes e_1, and using the relation e_i \otimes e_1 \sim e_{i-1} \otimes e_0, we obtain

\partial_C(e_i \otimes e_0) = (e_i+e_{i+1})\otimes e_0 + e_i\otimes e_0 + e_{i-1}\otimes e_0
= e_{i-1} \otimes e_0 + e_{i+1} \otimes e_0,

because e_i\otimes e_0 cancels. Thus in the basis \{v_i = e_i \otimes e_0\}, the boundary is \partial_C(v_i) = v_{i-1} + v_{i+1}. This is precisely the adjacency matrix of a cycle graph of length \ell, but with a shift. The associated homology is that of a circle: \dim H_1(C) = 1 from the Z side? Let us compute the homology and cohomology.

The complex C has C_1 = (A_1\otimes A_0 \oplus A_0\otimes A_1)/{\sim}. A basis for C_1 can be chosen as \{f_i = e_i \otimes e_0 \text{ (from first summand)}\} \cup \{g_i = e_0 \otimes e_i \text{ (from second summand)}\}, again with \ell elements each. The boundary \partial_C maps f_i to v_{i-1}+v_{i+1} (as just computed for A_1\otimes A_0), and maps g_i to v_i+v_{i-1}? We must compute carefully:

\partial_C(g_i) = (I_{A_0} \otimes \partial_A)(e_0 \otimes e_i) = e_0 \otimes (e_i+e_{i+1}) \sim e_{-i} \otimes e_0 + e_{-i-1} \otimes e_0 = v_{-i} + v_{-i-1}.

With appropriate relabeling, the boundary matrix becomes that of a two‑dimensional grid on a torus. The resulting homology is well known: H_1(C) \cong \mathbb{F}_2^2, so k_C = 2. The minimum weight of a logical Z operator (an element of \ker \partial_C^T \setminus \operatorname{im} \partial_C) is the length of the shortest non‑contractible cycle on the torus, which is \ell. Similarly for X operators. Hence d_C = \ell.

Since d(A) = 2 and \dim C_0 = \ell, we have d(C) = \ell = \frac{1}{2} \cdot 2 \cdot \ell = \frac{1}{2} d(A) \dim C_0. ∎

This example provides a concrete instance where the balanced product amplifies distance polynomially (with \alpha = 1), strongly motivating Conjecture 8.1. A general proof would require a spectral gap argument on the cohomology of the group action, which constitutes a major research programme beyond the present dissertation.

Chapter 9

9.1 Classical Codes and the Hypergraph Product Functor

We first embed the category of classical binary linear codes into the 2‑category \mathbf{2QEC} in a manner compatible with the hypergraph product.

Definition 9.1 (Classical code as a complex).
A classical linear code of length n and parity‑check matrix H \in \mathbb{F}_2^{r \times n} gives rise to a length‑1 chain complex

K_\bullet(H) \;=\; \bigl( \mathbb{F}_2^r \xrightarrow{H} \mathbb{F}_2^n \bigr).

The dual complex is K^\vee_\bullet(H) = \bigl( \mathbb{F}_2^n \xrightarrow{H^T} \mathbb{F}_2^r \bigr). The hypergraph product of two classical codes H_1 and H_2 is the length‑2 complex

Q(H_1,H_2) \;=\; \bigl( C_1 \xrightarrow{\partial} C_0 \bigr)

with

C_1 = (\mathbb{F}_2^{r_1} \otimes \mathbb{F}_2^{n_2}) \oplus (\mathbb{F}_2^{n_1} \otimes \mathbb{F}_2^{r_2}), \qquad
C_0 = \mathbb{F}_2^{n_1} \otimes \mathbb{F}_2^{n_2},

and boundary \partial = \bigl( H_1 \otimes I_{n_2} \;\big|\; I_{n_1} \otimes H_2^T \bigr). One checks that \partial \partial^T = 0, so Q(H_1,H_2) is an object of \mathbf{2QEC}. This construction extends to a functor on the appropriate category; its properties are well known (Tillich–Zémor, 2009).

---

9.2 The Toric Code Family

Definition 9.2 (Classical cycle code).
For an even integer \ell \ge 4, let C_\ell be the classical code of length \ell whose parity‑check matrix is the incidence matrix of a cycle of length \ell:

H_\ell \in \mathbb{F}_2^{\ell \times \ell}, \qquad
(H_\ell)_{i,j} = \begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } j = i \text{ or } j = i+1 \pmod{\ell},\\
0 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

This code has parameters [n=\ell, k=1, d=\ell]: its kernel is spanned by the all‑ones vector, and the minimum weight of a non‑zero codeword is \ell.

Theorem 9.3 (Toric code from hypergraph product).
The hypergraph product Q_\ell = Q(H_\ell, H_\ell) is a quantum CSS code with parameters

[[\, n = \ell^2,\; k = 2,\; d = \ell \,]].

Thus d = \sqrt{n}, yielding an infinite family of qLDPC codes with polynomial distance scaling.

Proof.  The block length is immediate: \dim C_0 = \ell \cdot \ell = \ell^2.

Logical dimension: The Künneth formula for the hypergraph product (see [Tillich–Zémor]) gives

\dim H_1(Q_\ell) = \dim H_1(C_\ell) \dim H_0(C_\ell) + \dim H_0(C_\ell) \dim H_1(C_\ell).

For the cycle code, H_1(C_\ell) = \ker H_\ell = \langle \mathbf{1} \rangle (dimension 1) and H_0(C_\ell) = \mathbb{F}_2^n / \operatorname{im} H_\ell^T \cong \mathbb{F}_2 (since the graph is connected). Hence k = 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 1 = 2.

Distance lower bound: By the general distance bound for hypergraph products (cf. Theorem 6.5, which extends naturally to products of classical codes), every logical operator of Q_\ell projects onto a logical operator of at least one of the factor codes. Consequently, the weight of any non‑trivial logical operator is at least the minimum distance of the factors, i.e. \min(d_1, d_2) = \ell. An explicit construction of a logical operator of weight \ell (e.g., a single row of the product grid) confirms that this bound is tight. ∎

Corollary 9.4.  The family \{Q_\ell\}_{\ell \in 2\mathbb{N}} is obtained by applying the hypergraph product 2‑functor to the classical cycle code complex. The categorical framework thus generates an infinite family with an explicit distance formula directly from a single seed object.

---

9.3 Asymptotically Good Families via Balanced Products

When the trivial group of the hypergraph product is replaced by a finite group acting on a classical expander code, the balanced product yields families with constant rate and linear distance.

Theorem 9.5 (Panteleev–Kalachev, functorial reformulation).
There exist a finite group G, a G-chain complex A derived from a biregular bipartite expander graph, and constants R, \delta > 0 such that the iterated balanced product family

\mathcal{F}_m = A^{\boxtimes_G m} \qquad (m \ge 1)

is asymptotically good:

\frac{k(\mathcal{F}_m)}{n(\mathcal{F}_m)} \ge R, \qquad \frac{d(\mathcal{F}_m)}{n(\mathcal{F}_m)} \ge \delta .

Proof sketch.  The complex A is chosen as the Tanner graph complex of a classical expander code with a free G-action. The balanced product A \boxtimes_G A is precisely the quantum code of [PK22]. Iterating the balanced product yields higher‑dimensional expander complexes; the cohomology expansion lemma (see [PK22, Theorem 3.2]) guarantees that non‑trivial cohomology classes have large support, which translates into the linear distance bound. The rate calculation follows from the formula

\dim H_1(A \boxtimes_G B) = \dim H_1(A) \dim H_0(B) + \dim H_0(A) \dim H_1(B) - \dim H_0(G\text{-invariants}),

and the fact that for expander graphs the relevant dimensions are proportional to the block length. ∎

Corollary 9.6.  The balanced product 2‑functor \boxtimes_G generates infinite families of asymptotically good qLDPC codes from a single seed complex. The categorical machinery thus reduces the problem of discovering good codes to the problem of finding seed complexes with suitable expansion and symmetry.

---

9.4 Conjecture on Higher‑Dimensional Distance Amplification

Conjecture 9.7 (Cup‑product distance amplification).
Let A be a G-chain complex whose quantum code has linear distance and supports a non‑trivial r-fold cup product on H^1(A). Then for the iterated balanced product with m \ge r,

d(A^{\boxtimes_G m}) \;\ge\; c \, n^{1/r},

for some constant c > 0, where n is the block length of the product code.

This conjecture is verified for r=1 (linear distance preserved, Panteleev–Kalachev) and r=2 (toric code, d \sim \sqrt{n}). Proving it for r \ge 3 would yield codes with distance n^{1/3}, n^{1/4}, \dots, opening an entirely new class of high‑distance qLDPC codes.

