Chapter 10

Compositional Fault‑Tolerant Architectures

In this concluding chapter we prove a general composition theorem showing that any well‑typed diagram in \mathbf{2QEC} defines a fault‑tolerant quantum circuit whose parameters are guaranteed by the types of its components. We then apply this result to give a categorical formulation of universal gate sets via code switching, and we argue that the framework scales to distributed quantum infrastructure.

---

10.1 The Composition Theorem

Definition 10.1 (Code diagram).
A code diagram \mathcal{D} is a finite directed graph whose vertices v are labelled by objects C_v \in \mathbf{2QEC} together with a typing derivation \vdash_v C_v : \operatorname{\texttt{Code}}(n_v, k_v, d_v), and whose edges e : v \to w are labelled by 1‑morphisms f_e : C_v \to C_w together with a typing derivation \vdash_e f_e : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q_v, Q_w). The diagram is well‑typed if all vertices and edges carry such derivations.

Theorem 10.2 (Compositional fault tolerance).
Let \mathcal{D} be a well‑typed code diagram and let v_0, v_\infty be vertices such that there exists a directed path \gamma from v_0 to v_\infty with composite 1‑morphism f_\gamma. Then:

1. k_{v_\infty} \ge k_{v_0} and d_{v_\infty} \ge d_{v_0}.
2. Any physical circuit implementing f_\gamma by composing the circuits of the edges along \gamma is fault‑tolerant: it maps the logical subspace of C_{v_0} into that of C_{v_\infty} and suppresses errors up to weight \lfloor (d_{v_0}-1)/2 \rfloor.
3. If two parallel paths \gamma, \gamma' are related by a 2‑morphism \eta : f_\gamma \Rightarrow f_{\gamma'} in \mathbf{2QEC}, then the corresponding circuits are fault‑tolerantly equivalent.

Proof.  (1) The typing derivation for each edge e asserts the inequalities k_{\text{target}} \ge k_{\text{source}} and d_{\text{target}} \ge d_{\text{source}}. By induction on the length of \gamma, these inequalities compose, yielding the claim.

(2) Each edge morphism f_e : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(Q_v, Q_w) is, by the soundness of the type system (Theorem 5.3), a chain map that injects the homology of the source into that of the target and preserves the distance. The composition of such maps along \gamma is again a chain map with the same properties; a fault‑tolerant circuit implementing the composition exists because each constituent morphism is realisable by a fault‑tolerant protocol (lattice surgery, code deformation, etc.).

(3) The existence of \eta : f_\gamma \Rightarrow f_{\gamma'} means the two chain maps are chain homotopic. The conversion rule (Conv) of the type system (Chapter 5) ensures that the typing derivation of f_\gamma can be transported to f_{\gamma'}. Physically, the chain homotopy corresponds to a sequence of stabiliser operations and local unitaries that transforms the circuit for f_\gamma into that for f_{\gamma'} without altering the logical action. ∎

Corollary 10.3 (Modular verification).
The fault‑tolerance of a composite quantum architecture modelled as a code diagram \mathcal{D} is guaranteed by the local typing of its vertices and edges. Formal verification reduces to checking the dependent types of the constituent 1‑morphisms, which can be automated using the Lean 4 formalisation of Chapters 5–6.

---

10.2 Universality via Code Switching

The Eastin–Knill theorem prohibits a single code from admitting a universal set of transversal gates. The categorical framework resolves this by distributing the gate set across different codes and functorially shuttling logical qubits between them.

Definition 10.4 (Gate‑switching diagram).
A gate‑switching diagram consists of a storage code S \in \mathbf{2QEC} (optimised for distance), a computational code C \in \mathbf{TransQEC} (Chapter 7) admitting a transversal non‑Clifford gate G, and a pair of typed 1‑morphisms

f_{\text{out}} : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(S, C), \qquad
f_{\text{in}} : \operatorname{\texttt{Morph}}(C, S),

such that the round‑trip f_{\text{in}} \circ f_{\text{out}} is homotopic to \operatorname{id}_S on the logical subspace (i.e. there exists a 2‑morphism \eta : f_{\text{in}} \circ f_{\text{out}} \Rightarrow \operatorname{id}_S).

Theorem 10.5 (Transversal universality via switching).
The gate‑switching diagram implements the transversal gate G on the storage code S in a fault‑tolerant manner. By iterating with a set of computational codes that together provide a universal set, one obtains a universal fault‑tolerant gate set on S.

Proof.  The composed morphism

f_{\text{in}} \circ G \circ f_{\text{out}} : S \longrightarrow S

is well‑typed because G is an endo‑1‑morphism on C preserving its parameters, and f_{\text{out}}, f_{\text{in}} are typed morphisms. The round‑trip homotopy guarantees that this composite acts as G on the logical level. The physical implementation consists of shuttling the logical qubit from S to C, applying the transversal G on C, and shuttling back; each step is fault‑tolerant by Theorem 10.2. Repeating with a computational code for each generator yields a universal set. ∎

---

10.3 Scaling to Distributed Quantum Infrastructure

For a quantum network, each node runs a local code, and edges are physical channels implementing code morphisms. The composition theorem ensures that the whole network inherits the fault‑tolerance of its constituents.

Definition 10.6 (Homogeneous quantum fabric).
A quantum memory fabric of scale N is a code diagram \mathcal{D}_N whose vertices are labelled by codes from a single family generated by iterating a balanced product \boxtimes_G from a fixed seed complex A, and whose edges are drawn from a fixed finite set of interconnection morphisms.

Theorem 10.7 (Scalability of the fabric).
If the seed complex A yields an asymptotically good family, then the fabric \mathcal{D}_N provides a total logical capacity K = \Theta(N) and end‑to‑end distance D = \Theta(\operatorname{diam}(\mathcal{D}_N)). Moreover, the formal verification of the entire fabric reduces to the verification of the seed A and the finitely many interconnection morphisms.

Proof.  The vertex codes are of the form A^{\boxtimes_G m}, whose parameters scale as in Theorem 9.5. The edges preserve distance by construction. The composition theorem (Theorem 10.2) applied to any pair of paths yields the stated bounds. The verification reduction follows from functoriality: once the seed and the functors are proved correct in Lean, the correctness of all composites is automatically derivable by induction. ∎

---

10.4 Concluding Remarks

This dissertation has constructed a strict 2‑category \mathbf{2QEC} whose objects are CSS stabiliser codes, whose 1‑morphisms are fault‑tolerant code transformations, and whose 2‑morphisms are equivalences of transformation pathways. We have built a faithful 2‑functor from classical Tanner graphs into this category, proved distance preservation for the hypergraph product, shown that transversal non‑Clifford gates are inherited under balanced products with cup products, and formulated a precise conjecture on functorial distance amplification.

The central thesis of this work is that quantum code discovery can be formulated as navigation through a higher category of fault‑tolerant structures. The mathematical machinery erected here substantiates that thesis and provides the formal foundations for a modular, verifiable quantum cognitive infrastructure. The open problems laid out—the classification of \mathbf{TransQEC}, the proof of the distance amplification conjecture, and the complete formalisation of \mathbf{2QEC}_{\text{Lean}}—sketch a rich research programme at the intersection of category theory, quantum information, and formal verification.


