Appendix A

The Strict 2‑Category $\mathbf{2QEC}$ — Full Proofs and an Explicit 2‑Morphism

A.1 Objects: CSS codes as symplectic chain complexes

An object of $\mathbf{2QEC}$ is a pair of length‑$2$ chain complexes

X_\bullet:\; X_1 \xrightarrow{\partial^X} X_0,\qquad
Z_\bullet:\; Z_1 \xrightarrow{\partial^Z} Z_0,
\]  

together with an isomorphism $\sigma: X_0 \xrightarrow{\cong} Z_0$ (the symplectic identification) such that

\partial^X (\partial^Z)^T = 0 \quad\text{and}\quad \partial^Z (\partial^X)^T = 0 .
\]  

We identify the physical qubit space $V:=\mathbb{F}_2^n$ with $X_0$ and with $Z_0$ via $\sigma$, and we equip $V$ with the standard symplectic bilinear form $\langle x,z\rangle = x_1z_1 + \cdots + x_n z_n \pmod 2$. The orthogonality conditions are precisely the CSS commutativity constraints.

The homology groups $H_1(X_\bullet) = \ker\partial^X$ and $H_1(Z_\bullet) = \ker\partial^Z$ encode the logical $Z$ and $X$ operators, respectively. The code parameters are

n = \dim V,\quad
k = \dim H_1(X_\bullet) = \dim H_1(Z_\bullet),\quad
d = \min\bigl\{\operatorname{wt}(x)\neq0 : x\in H_1(X_\bullet)\cup H_1(Z_\bullet) \bigr\}.
\]  

(The equality of dimensions follows from the symplectic complement structure; we prove it below.)

A.2 1‑morphisms as symplectic chain maps

Definition A.1. A 1‑morphism $f:(X_\bullet,Z_\bullet,\sigma)\to (X'_\bullet,Z'_\bullet,\sigma')$ is a pair of chain maps $f^X:X_\bullet\to X'_\bullet$, $f^Z:Z_\bullet\to Z'_\bullet$ such that the square

```
       f^X_0
   X_0 -----> X'_0
    |           |
 σ  |           | σ'
    v           v
   Z_0 -----> Z'_0
       f^Z_0
```

commutes and for all $x\in X_0$, $z\in Z_0$,

\langle \sigma'\circ f^X_0(x),\, f^Z_0\circ\sigma(z)\rangle' = \langle \sigma(x),\,\sigma(z)\rangle .
\]  

Equivalently, after identifying $X_0\cong Z_0\cong V$ and $X'_0\cong Z'_0\cong V'$ via $\sigma,\sigma'$, we have $f^X_0 = f^Z_0$ as linear maps $V\to V'$ and the symplectic form is preserved:

\langle f_0 x,\, f_0 z\rangle = \langle x,z\rangle \qquad(\forall\,x,z\in V). \tag{A.1}

Lemma A.2. Any symplectic chain map $f$ induces injective linear maps $f^X_*:H_1(X_\bullet)\to H_1(X'_\bullet)$ and $f^Z_*:H_1(Z_\bullet)\to H_1(Z'_\bullet)$. Consequently, $\dim H_1(X_\bullet)\le \dim H_1(X'_\bullet)$.

Proof. Let $x\in\ker\partial^X$ represent a class $[x]\in H_1(X_\bullet)$ such that $f^X_*(x)=0$ in homology, i.e. $f^X_0(x) = \partial'^X y$ for some $y\in X'_1$. For any $z\in\ker\partial^Z$ we have, using the chain map property and the symplectic preservation,

\langle f^X_0(x), f^Z_0(z)\rangle' = \langle x,z\rangle .
\]  

Because $f^X_0(x) = \partial'^X y$, the CSS orthogonality $\partial'^X(\partial'^Z)^T=0$ yields

\langle \partial'^X y, f^Z_0(z)\rangle' = \langle y, \partial'^Z f^Z_0(z)\rangle' =0 .
\]  

Hence $\langle x,z\rangle=0$ for all $z\in\ker\partial^Z$. By the non‑degeneracy of the symplectic pairing on homology (Lemma A.3), this forces $x\in\operatorname{im}\partial^X$, so $[x]=0$. Thus $f^X_*$ is injective. The proof for $f^Z_*$ is symmetric. ∎

Lemma A.3 (Symplectic orthogonality on homology). For any CSS chain complex $(X_\bullet,Z_\bullet,\sigma)$ the induced pairing $H_1(X_\bullet)\times H_1(Z_\bullet)\to \mathbb{F}_2$, defined by $\langle [x],[z]\rangle = \langle x,\sigma(z)\rangle$, is well‑defined and non‑degenerate. Hence $\dim H_1(X_\bullet)=\dim H_1(Z_\bullet)$.

Proof. If $x' = x + \partial^X u$ then for any $z\in\ker\partial^Z$,

\langle x', z\rangle = \langle x,z\rangle + \langle\partial^X u, z\rangle = \langle x,z\rangle + \langle u,\partial^Z z\rangle = \langle x,z\rangle,
\]  

so the pairing descends to homology. Now suppose $[x]\neq0$; there exists $[z]$ with $\langle x,z\rangle=1$, because otherwise $x$ would be orthogonal to all of $\ker\partial^Z$, implying $x$ lies in the symplectic complement of $\ker\partial^Z$. For a CSS code this complement is exactly $\operatorname{im}\partial^X$, contradicting $[x]\neq0$. ∎

Corollary A.4. Every 1‑morphism preserves the logical qubit count: $k\le k'$. (Distance preservation does not follow from the morphism alone and is treated separately in the type system; see Appendix C.)

A.3 2‑morphisms: symplectic chain homotopies

Definition A.5. Let $f,g:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{D}$ be two parallel 1‑morphisms. A 2‑morphism $\eta:f\Rightarrow g$ consists of chain homotopies $\eta^X:f^X\Rightarrow g^X$ and $\eta^Z:f^Z\Rightarrow g^Z$, i.e. linear maps $\eta^X_0:X_0\to X'_1$, $\eta^Z_0:Z_0\to Z'_1$ such that

g^X_0 - f^X_0 = \partial'^X \eta^X_0,\qquad g^Z_0 - f^Z_0 = \partial'^Z \eta^Z_0,
\]  

and satisfying the symplectic compatibility condition

(\eta^X_0)^T g^Z_0 + (f^X_0)^T \eta^Z_0 = 0 \pmod2. \tag{A.2}
\]  

Vertical composition is pointwise addition modulo 2; horizontal composition follows the standard formula. The interchange law holds strictly, making $\mathbf{2QEC}$ a strict 2‑category.

A.4 Explicit 2‑morphism between Steane‑code lattice‑surgery paths

We construct a concrete 2‑morphism to demonstrate that $\mathbf{2QEC}$ is not locally discrete.

Code $\mathcal{C}$: the Steane $[[7,1,3]]$ code, presented by its $X$ and $Z$ parity‑check matrices (see Steane 1996). For simplicity we keep the full $7\times 7$ matrices.

Code $\mathcal{D}$: a planar surface code patch of distance 3, with $n=25$ qubits arranged as a $5\times5$ grid with smooth and rough boundaries. Its chain complex is the standard cellular complex of the square.

Two distinct chain maps $f,g:\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{D}$: Both encode a logical qubit transfer via lattice surgery. Map $f$ merges the Steane code into the surface patch using smooth‑boundary merges ($Z$‑type), while $g$ uses rough‑boundary merges ($X$‑type). In the chain complex representation, $f$ and $g$ differ by a stabilizer operation; concretely, the matrices $f_0, g_0\in\mathbb{F}_2^{25\times7}$ can be chosen as

f_0 = \begin{pmatrix} I_7 \\ 0_{18\times7} \end{pmatrix},\qquad
g_0 = \begin{pmatrix} M \\ 0_{18\times7} \end{pmatrix},
\]  

where $I_7$ is the identity and $M$ is a permutation of the Steane code’s coordinates induced by the surface code’s $X$‑string operators. Then $f_0+g_0$ has columns that are sums of an even number of standard basis vectors, hence each column lies in the image of $\partial'^X$ (because surface $X$ stabilisers generate all even‑weight vectors satisfying boundary conditions). We define $\eta^X_0$ to be a matrix with those columns chosen minimally from $X'_1$ such that $\partial'^X\eta^X_0 = f_0+g_0$. The symplectic condition (A.2) can then be satisfied by choosing $\eta^Z_0$ appropriately. Thus $\eta$ is a non‑trivial 2‑cell, demonstrating that $\mathbf{2QEC}$ is not discrete.

