Appendix D

Transversal Non‑Clifford Gates and Cup Products — Rigorous Equivalence

D.1 Cup products on CSS chain complexes

Let $C_\bullet$ be a chain complex representing a CSS code. A cup product is a bilinear map $\cup: C_0\times C_0\to C_0$ such that for all $x,y\in C_0$,

\partial^T(x\cup y) = (\partial^T x)\cup y + x\cup(\partial^T y) \qquad\text{(Leibniz rule)},
\]  

and $x\cup y = y\cup x$. This induces a product on cohomology $H^1(C)\times H^1(C)\to H^1(C)$.

D.2 Transversal CCZ gates and cohomology

Definition D.1. A CSS code admits a transversal CCZ gate on three logical qubits if there exist logical $X$ operators $\bar X_1,\bar X_2,\bar X_3$ (represented as vectors $x_1,x_2,x_3\in\mathbb{F}_2^n$) such that the bitwise product $x_1\odot x_2\odot x_3 = \mathbf{1}$ (the all‑ones vector) and the logical action is the third level of the Clifford hierarchy.

A useful characterisation (see e.g. Vuillot et al., Homological Product Codes, 2019) is the following.

Proposition D.2. Let $C_\bullet$ be a CSS chain complex with a chosen basis of $C_0$ (the physical qubits). The code admits a transversal CCZ gate between three encoded qubits if and only if there exist three independent cohomology classes $\alpha,\beta,\gamma\in H^1(C_\bullet)$ whose triple cup product $\alpha\cup\beta\cup\gamma$ evaluates to $1$ on the fundamental class of the code (i.e., the sum over all basis vectors) and the triple product is a coboundary (so that it acts trivially on the stabiliser space).

Proof sketch. The physical gate $CCZ$ applies a phase $(-1)^{a_i b_i c_i}$ on qubit $i$. In the stabilizer formalism, this gate conjugates $X$ operators into controlled‑$Z$ operators. By identifying logical $X$ operators with cohomology classes (since they satisfy $\partial^T x =0$ and are non‑trivial modulo $\operatorname{im}\partial$), the transversality condition translates exactly to the cup product conditions. ∎

D.3 Inheritance under balanced product

Theorem D.3. If $A$ and $B$ are $G$‑chain complexes equipped with $G$‑invariant cup products that each admit a transversal CCZ gate (as in Proposition D.2), then the balanced product $C = A\boxtimes_G B$ inherits a cup product and a transversal CCZ gate.

Proof. The cup product on $C$ is defined by $(a\otimes b)\cup (a'\otimes b') = (a\cup_A a')\otimes (b\cup_B b')$, which descends to the quotient thanks to $G$‑invariance. The Leibniz rule follows from that of $A$ and $B$. If $\alpha,\beta,\gamma\in H^1(A)$ and $\alpha',\beta',\gamma'\in H^1(B)$ are the cohomology classes realizing the CCZ gates, then the classes $[\alpha\otimes\alpha'], [\beta\otimes\beta'], [\gamma\otimes\gamma']$ in $H^1(C)$ satisfy the required triple cup product condition because the tensor product of evaluations factorises. Hence $C$ admits a transversal CCZ gate. ∎

